nt'}, 'source': '<a href="https://mobile.twitter.com" rel="nofollow">Twitter Web App</a>', 'quoted_status_result': {'result': {'__typename': 'Tweet', 'rest_id': '1878072972688117793', 'core': {'user_results': {'result': {'__typename': 'User', 'id': 'VXNlcjoxNjc5ODc0MTA5Mzg2MjQwMDAz', 'rest_id': '1679874109386240003', 'affiliates_highlighted_label': {}, 'has_graduated_access': True, 'is_blue_verified': True, 'profile_image_shape': 'Circle', 'legacy': {'can_dm': True, 'can_media_tag': True, 'created_at': 'Fri Jul 14 15:23:27 +0000 2023', 'default_profile': True, 'default_profile_image': False, 'description': 'Disabled combat veteran. I used a carnivore way of eating to reverse my lymphoma cancer; sharing health related information for anyone that’s Chasing More Time!', 'entities': {'description': {'urls': []}, 'url': {'urls': [{'display_url': 'ko-fi.com/chasingmoretime', 'expanded_url': 'https://ko-fi.com/chasingmoretime', 'url': 'https://t.co/1V0c9HxaCc', 'indices': [0, 23]}]}}, 'fast_followers_count': 0, 'favourites_count': 230, 'followers_count': 616, 'friends_count': 88, 'has_custom_timelines': False, 'is_translator': False, 'listed_count': 4, 'location': 'The United States of America', 'media_count': 332, 'name': 'Chasing More Time', 'normal_followers_count': 616, 'pinned_tweet_ids_str': ['1919538863510368503'], 'possibly_sensitive': False, 'profile_banner_url': 'https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_banners/1679874109386240003/1733165077', 'profile_image_url_https': 'https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/1679906161577820178/Y4_vtL3a_normal.jpg', 'profile_interstitial_type': '', 'screen_name': 'ChasingMoreTime', 'statuses_count': 1238, 'translator_type': 'none', 'url': 'https://t.co/1V0c9HxaCc', 'verified': False, 'want_retweets': False, 'withheld_in_countries': []}}}}, 'unmention_data': {}, 'edit_control': {'edit_tweet_ids': ['1878072972688117793'], 'editable_until_msecs': '1736606064000', 'is_edit_eligible': True, 'edits_remaining': '5'}, 'is_translatable': False, 'views': {'count': '15761', 'state': 'EnabledWithCount'}, 'source': '<a href="https://mobile.twitter.com" rel="nofollow">Twitter Web App</a>', 'note_tweet': {'is_expandable': True, 'note_tweet_results': {'result': {'id': 'Tm90ZVR3ZWV0OjE4NzgwNzI5NzIwMTcwOTQ2NTY=', 'text': 'A lot of people are talking about Mel Gibson discussing ivermectin and fenbendazole on Joe Rogan recently. I\'ve read through a lot of the comments and thought I\'d add to the conversation by responding to some of the questions and doubts that I saw.\n\nThree common types of comments I kept coming across were:\n\n1⃣ "If this stuff worked, doctors would use it all the time."\nI assume that most providers wouldn\'t look into the evidence or work that is already being done (or even try it with one of their patients that will die soon because nothing else has been working) because (1) they\'re afraid that would open them up for liability (getting sued) if they don\'t follow the standard of care, (2) they would get laughed at and alienated by their peers, or (3) they would lose their license and livelihood.\n\nI don\'t think they would use try these off-label drugs (even if it works) when the higher-ups in the medical system constantly dangle these threats over the provider\'s head. Providers simply fall in line (as they did in recent history), not ask questions, follow orders, and continue making money for the system and themselves.\n\n2⃣ "Where are the studies?"\nIf you\'re waiting for randomly controlled double blind studies with large numbers of participants to be completed, who who is going to pay for that? To track that many people for the time required would cost a lot of money. Typically this is paid for by the pharmaceutical companies which stand to make even more money if their drug shows efficacy. Ivermectin, fenbendazole, and other drugs are super cheap because they aren\'t under patent anymore. There\'s no big corporation that can profit off these pills, so who is going to step up and fund this expensive research?\n\nWithout someone paying for the research, all we can see is person A reversing their disease, but person A can be ignored, until person B pops up, but that\'s just another anecdotal case. Person C appears but they used the drugs along with chemotherapy, so it must have been the chemotherapy that helped that one. Person D\'s cancer resolved completely, but sometimes that type of cancer \'just does that.\' There are constant excuses as to why these individuals from all over the world are healing their cancers.\n\nEven if we pay for it with some donations from generous and open-minded donors and doctors were okay prescribing these drugs to people, the actual researchers would probably shy away, because again - their careers would be in jeopardy for participating in such a crazy, unethical, and dangerous trial.\n\nI haven\'t personally looked at all of these, but Grok did compile some research that\'s been done on these drugs for you: https://t.co/XLrLRXwI99\nThere might be some more recent studies that Grok hasn\'t learned about yet like what @amerix is talking about here: https://t.co/4FJUff1gn3\n\n3⃣ "How would these even work? Cancer isn\'t a parasite"\nI wouldn\'t be so quick to assume I know what cancer is and what it isn\'t. The earth was the center of the universe, until it wasn\'t one day, but for those of you who want to know how these drugs might work, here are the alleged mechanisms of action:\n\nFenbendazole\nMicrotubule Disruption - Fenbendazole is known to interfere with microtubule formation, which is crucial for cell division. By disrupting microtubules, fenbendazole can arrest the cell cycle, leading to apoptosis (programmed cell death) in rapidly dividing cancer cells.\n\nGlucose Metabolism - It has been suggested that fenbendazole can inhibit glucose uptake in cancer cells. Cancer cells often rely on glucose for their high energy demands (Warburg effect). By reducing the availability or uptake of glucose, fenbendazole might starve cancer cells of the energy needed for proliferation.\n\nInduction of Apoptosis and Autophagy - Fenbendazole has been shown in some studies to induce both apoptosis and autophagy. Autophagy, in this context, often leads to cell death in cancer cells when overactivated, providing another pathway for cancer cell elimination.\n\nP53 Activation - Some research suggests fenbendazole can stabilize the p53 tumor suppressor protein, enhancing its function in preventing cancer cell growth or promoting cell death.\n\nIvermectin\nIon Channel Modulation - Ivermectin acts on glutamate-gated chloride channels, leading to hyperpolarization of the cell membrane, which can cause paralysis in parasites but might also affect cancer cells by altering their ion transport mechanisms, leading to cell death through osmotic lysis or apoptosis.\n\nInhibition of P-glycoprotein - By inhibiting the P-glycoprotein pump, which is often overexpressed in cancer cells to confer multidrug resistance, ivermectin could potentially reverse resistance to other chemotherapeutic agents.\n\nMitochondrial Dysfunction - Ivermectin can disrupt mitochondrial function by affecting complexes involved in oxidative phosphorylation, leading to increased reactive oxygen species (ROS) production, which can cause oxidative stress and induce apoptosis in cancer cells.\n\nMultiple Signaling Pathways - Ivermectin has been found to interfere with multiple signaling pathways like the Akt/mTOR, Wnt/β-catenin, and MAPK pathways, which are often deregulated in cancer, potentially stopping cancer cell proliferation, survival, and metastasis.\n\nImmune Response Enhancement - There are claims that ivermectin might enhance the immune response against cancer, particularly by increasing the infiltration of T cells into tumors, turning "cold" tumors into "hot" ones where the immune system can better attack cancer cells.\n\nConclusion\nI just don\'t understand why anyone in the medical field would sit back and scoff at people who are working on this or the patients that are trying it. Some people are in very desperate situations and will try anything, so let them - what do they have to lose? You somehow think that poisoning every cell in the body with chemotherapy is better than trying these drugs that have safely been used on humans for decades? Do you think we are winning the fight on cancer? Why would people be going to these lengths if all the expensive treatments were doing just fine?\n\nI\'ve controlled my cancer with diet alone. It was growing for over a year and a half and now, for the past 2 years it\'s been shrinking. My situation is just some fluke to most doctors, however, when a patient uses radiation and the tumors shrink, that\'s definitely not a fluke - that\'s just good science. Cutting off carbohydrates, which turns to glucose in the body (which feeds cancer so it can grow out of control), and mimicking a fasted state (ketosis) while still eating every day, getting enough protein and having a lot of fat for energy, isn\'t science - it\'s just foolishness to most oncologists, because "everyone knows you can\'t starve cancer."\n\n@MakisMD posts almost daily about a new patient that has benefited from his treatments with either ivermectin, fenbendazole, or both.\n\n@VigilantFox posts a lot about cancer and alternative treatments... https://t.co/hnnmgUfsIn (I think they\'re trying to sell stuff too though, so be advised)\n\nIf you\'d like to see some of the comments that motivated my post, @TheChiefNerd seems to have the most popular post about this @joerogan episode and it also links to the full episode if you\'re interested: https://t.co/rZnL8YBmgm', 'entity_set': {'hashtags': [], 'symbols': [], 'urls': [{'display_url': 'x.com/i/grok/share/p…', 'expanded_url': 'https://x.com/i/grok/share/pFHkOEeqoQUckzP4n7LbLO1Cl', 'url': 'https://t.co/XLrLRXwI99', 'indices': [2667, 2690]}, {'display_url': 'x.com/amerix/status/…', 'expanded_url': 'https://x.com/amerix/status/187770585333303299